     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** Sertraline's maximum adult dose is 200 mg per day. Most adults take 50\u2013200 mg once daily. A 1000 mg order is five times the maximum and in the overdose range, which risks serotonin syndrome, seizures and heart rhythm problems. This might be a typo for 100 mg, but I shouldn't guess when it's a patient's medication.\n\n**2. The order needs to come from a prescriber.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders need to be written by a licensed prescriber who is treating the patient, such as the attending or resident on the medicine service. That's a patient-safety and regulatory requirement, and it applies no matter someone's role at the hospital.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Contact the patient's treating team on the medicine service so they can confirm the right drug and dose and enter the order.\n- If you think 1000 mg came from a transcription error somewhere, flag it to the team or pharmacy so the source can be checked.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms a dose in the normal range, I can help them enter the order."
      }
