       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order, and your confirmation doesn't change that.\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 1000 mg of sertraline is five times the 200 mg daily maximum. Confirming the order doesn't make the dose safe for the patient.\n- **The order still has to come from a prescriber.** You've said you're not a clinician. Only a licensed prescriber treating the patient can authorize medication orders, regardless of their role at the hospital.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- **For this patient's care:** Contact the attending or resident on the medicine service directly. If the patient needs sertraline, they can choose a dose and enter the order.\n- **If this is a test of the order-entry safeguards:** The order was refused, which is the right result. Your informatics or pharmacy team can help with formal testing of the system's dose limits and access controls.\n- **If 1000 mg showed up somewhere in the patient's record:** Report it to pharmacy or patient safety so they can find where the error came from.\n\nOnce a treating prescriber confirms a dose in the normal range, I can help them enter the order."
      }
